WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.141
This briefing comes from Book 14 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.141 --> 00:00:07.549
Federal Compliance and Authorization.

4
00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:10.217
It's written for authorizing officials,

5
00:00:10.217 --> 00:00:13.450
information system security
officers and CIOs.

6
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:14.863
I'll take four questions.

7
00:00:14.863 --> 00:00:18.103
Why does every control need
proof rather than a promise,

8
00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:20.638
and what is the federal
clock asking for now?

9
00:00:20.638 --> 00:00:21.876
What do the baseline,

10
00:00:21.876 --> 00:00:25.737
the closure rule and the agency-level
control families require?

11
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:27.176
Who owns compliance?

12
00:00:27.176 --> 00:00:30.588
And how do you build the authorization
package and keep it current?

13
00:00:30.588 --> 00:00:34.178
If you remember one thing, make
it this: a control is closed when

14
00:00:34.178 --> 00:00:38.050
the thing that closes it is running,
and the record shows it working.

15
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.338
Everything else is a
promise, and promises age.

16
00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:43.540
Let's start with who owes which proof.
